// AC operand order decided BELOW the operands' own head, in the theory echo.
//
// HS `fAppAC` (Term/Term/Raw.hs:119) sorts a flattened AC chain's operands
// with the derived `Ord (Term a)`, and `data Term a = LIT a | FAPP FunSym
// [Term a]` (Term/Term/Raw.hs:73-75) makes that comparison structural:
// constructor first — every `LIT` before every `FAPP` — then the head symbol,
// then the argument list elementwise.  Both operands of each `++` below are
// `pair`-headed, so neither the constructor nor the head decides and the
// comparison has to descend.
//
//   - `Depth` decides one level down: at argument 1 the 2-tuple holds a `LIT`
//     and the 3-tuple an `FAPP`, so `<a, z>` comes first;
//   - `NotLength` puts the same 3-tuple against `<a, <z, z>>`, whose argument 1
//     is an `FAPP` too.  The comparison descends a second level, `b` before
//     `z`, and the 3-tuple comes FIRST — operand size is not what orders them.
//
// `<a, b, c>` and `<a, z, z>` are also the flattened spelling HS `prettyTerm`
// prints for a right-nested `pair` chain (`split`, Term/Term.hs:313,323-324),
// so the echo shows three operands where the order was read off two: the
// printer's flattening is not the tree `Ord` walks.
//
// No corpus theory puts two same-headed `pair` chains in one AC chain.
//
// Compared slices: the whole `theory … end` echo (see check.sh), and the wf
// report block, which pins the run clean — these shapes reach no wf check.

theory PairEchoOrder
begin

builtins: multiset

rule Depth:
  [ In(a), In(b), In(c), In(z) ] --[ ]-> [ Out( <a,b,c> ++ <a,z> ) ]

rule NotLength:
  [ In(a), In(b), In(c), In(z) ] --[ ]-> [ Out( <a,b,c> ++ <a,<z,z>> ) ]

end
